findings.body:
Commit 931ebb7 (`fix(land): … its ancestry from scratch`) makes two promises for the pin-to-head check. It runs from the scratch git dir, and it runs with `core.commitGraph=false`, "so a forged commit graph cannot answer it" (ADR 0164).

Only the ancestry-failure test sees this git call. Its interceptor matches on `args.includes('merge-base')` and throws without looking at the other arguments:

```ts
fn((args, opts) => {
  if (args.includes('merge-base')) throw failure;
  return git(args, opts);
}),
```

Now take the mutant `git(['merge-base', '--is-ancestor', pin, head], …)` in `descendsFrom` (`src/core/land.service.ts:427`). It keeps the scratch dir but drops the commit-graph pin, and every test still passes:
- the happy path and the non-descendant refusal, which both run real git on a repo with no commit graph;
- this failure test;
- `land-refusals.test.ts`'s kill test, where `head === sha`, so `descendsFrom` never runs.

No other test in the repo mentions `commitGraph` for land.

That mutant reopens the hole the fix closed: a session writes a forged `objects/info/commit-graph`, and a rewritten head passes as a descendant of the pin. To catch it, record the `merge-base` argv in the interceptor and assert it contains `-c core.commitGraph=false`. `merge-gate.test.ts:1794` already does this:

```ts
expect(read.join(' ')).toContain('-c core.commitGraph=false log …')
```

`mergedOnto`'s commit-graph pin has no test either, so the same assertion would be worth adding there.

